# CODEOWNERS — who may touch the clean-room boundary
#
# The clean-room claim (docs/CLEANROOM.md section 2) is a process claim, and a
# process claim is only auditable if the diff cannot straddle the boundary by
# accident. This file makes the boundary a review gate rather than a promise.
#
# Two roles, separated by knowledge:
#
#   specification author -> writes specs/**, reads public docs + Apache-2.0 source
#   implementer          -> writes crates/cleanroom/**, reads the spec + prior art
#
# Neither role may read rxdb-premium in any form. specs/READING-LIST.md records
# who read what, per crate, and a PR that changes a clean-room crate must fill in
# the provenance question in .github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md.
#
# The dxdb-cleanroom team is the review gate. Members are added by the leader
# after reading specs/READING-LIST.md and confirming they have not seen the
# closed source. An implementer who has seen it does not work on the matching
# crate; they can work on the Apache-2.0 port instead (CLEANROOM.md section 2).

# Clean-room specifications: behavioural contracts written from public docs.
# Review here is about sufficiency and sourcing, not about code.
/specs/                          @Apothic-AI/dxdb-cleanroom

# Clean-room implementation crates. Nothing outside this tree may import them
# except the JS surface crate, and nothing here may import rxdb-premium.
/crates/cleanroom/               @Apothic-AI/dxdb-cleanroom

# The gap register is the published list of where we diverge from the paid
# features. It is a deliverable, so it gets the same gate as the code.
/docs/CLEANROOM-GAPS.md          @Apothic-AI/dxdb-cleanroom

# The governing documents are leader-owned (docs/WORKTREES.md). Listed here so a
# clean-room change to the rules themselves cannot slip through unreviewed.
/docs/CLEANROOM.md               @Apothic-AI/dxdb-leaders
/docs/lanes/m7-cleanroom.md      @Apothic-AI/dxdb-leaders

# The PR template carries the provenance question. Changing it changes the gate.
/.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md @Apothic-AI/dxdb-leaders
